1|0@fivenightsatfreddys^1438868335#cttj6lq:TomAtTARDIS$:When I first saw FNAF, I thought it wouldn't work as a horror game since you could always see where your enemies were, thus killing the scariness of the game. But then after watching some more gameplay, I realised that you had limited power and that the cameras used up more of this power supply the longer you watched them for, which meant that you couldn't just stare at cameras for 6 in-game hours. There was also the Foxy animatronic who could run up to your office at any given moment, which meant that you would have to take your eyes off of the other characters at points so you could keep tabs on Foxy. So I decided to give the game a chance, and I've loved it ever since :) Then of course, I started to notice that people were doing lots of theories on some secret plot buried within the game, and that hidden lore enriched the game even more for me, and that's one of the things I liked the most about FNAF1. You could either just play it as a straight horror game with a creepy atmosphere and solid gameplay, or you could also go clue-hunting as you played the game and try to find the secret story buried underneath the surface.
